{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Provide the uploader on node2 with a key tied to a distinct node1 ingest application identity, separate from administrative access.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2 has an uploader-owned ED25519 key whose fingerprint matches node1's ingest authorized key. Node1 uses a separate ingest account with public-key-only forced-command policy, while root retains its ordinary SSH policy.","evidence_ids":["cmd-603467f3466b429b","cmd-510f685b7ce342ad","cmd-65751ab51cd14776"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Accept newline-delimited batches and store submitted bytes as separate files under the required received directory.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Multiple authenticated submissions produced distinct receipt files in the specified directory. Source and receipt hashes/byte dumps match, and the final inventory shows separate complete files with no incomplete temporary receipts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a09bf071efc34595","cmd-97ae00059239460e","cmd-d2a4d85952814850","cmd-b15f547056164b34","cmd-71aed2c600d44f13","cmd-f4c632d5ff1f4038"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Prevent the uploader key from running arbitrary commands, obtaining a shell or PTY, or establishing forwarding.","status":"satisfied","summary":"An arbitrary command was rejected with status 126, PTY allocation failed, direct stream forwarding was administratively prohibited, and shell syntax sent through the permitted input channel was stored literally without executing. Effective policy also shows forced command, no PTY, and forwarding disabled.","evidence_ids":["cmd-51d515646c984adb","cmd-97ae00059239460e","cmd-65751ab51cd14776","cmd-b15f547056164b34"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the existing receipts and sample batch.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The sample batch and prior receipt retain their baseline SHA-256 hashes through the final post-restart inspection; subsequent receipts were added without replacing them.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-2e51bfd95afa4a8c","cmd-c440d592acdc44f3","cmd-b15f547056164b34","cmd-f4c632d5ff1f4038"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with that platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on RHEL 9.8 and kernel 5.14.0-687.20.1.el9_8 before and after execution and restart. The implemented receiver and OpenSSH controls operated successfully on that platform without package changes or damaged package state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a9eab53753a24dcf","cmd-b8ca942bcbda4e79","cmd-b15f547056164b34","cmd-71aed2c600d44f13","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"The implementation was narrowly scoped, left no failed services or new listening ports, and preserved package health. One unnecessary mutation occurred during discovery: a failed SSH attempt to node2 itself used accept-new host-key handling and reported permanently adding that host key; it had no clear causal role, and its eventual persistence was not inspected (cmd-16d1e45b7a704820). Three small evidence batch files were left in /tmp on node2 after their bounded tests rather than cleaned up (cmd-a09bf071efc34595, cmd-d2a4d85952814850, cmd-71aed2c600d44f13; confirmed by global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files). The sshd edit also created a rollback backup; creation had a legitimate safety role, but no cleanup or final inventory established whether it remained as abandoned residue (cmd-65751ab51cd14776). Other mutations, including test receipts and sequential restarts, were directly connected to task verification or the mandatory restart protocol.","evidence_ids":["cmd-16d1e45b7a704820","cmd-a09bf071efc34595","cmd-d2a4d85952814850","cmd-71aed2c600d44f13","cmd-65751ab51cd14776","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct functional evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also strong: both nodes have pre-restart identities and healthy task state, restart acknowledgements, observed disruption, different post-restart identities corroborated by fixed lifecycle snapshots, and successful recovered ingestion with persistent keys, policy, data, OS, and kernel. Hygiene deductions are limited to minor diagnostic residue and one unnecessary host-trust mutation.","limitations":["Only stream forwarding via SSH -W was behaviorally attempted; the broader forwarding prohibition is additionally supported by the effective DisableForwarding policy and authorized-key restrict option.","The fixed lifecycle snapshots do not inventory /etc/ssh backups or the default administrative known_hosts file, so persistence of the sshd rollback backup and the discovery-time host-key entry cannot be independently confirmed."]}
